Licences of the code in the dseams source package

dseams itself, meaning the R code, the C and C++ glue in src/*.c and
src/*.cpp, configure, cleanup and tools/, is under the MIT licence; see
LICENSE. The d-SEAMS engine copied into src/seams is also MIT, by the
d-SEAMS core team, except src/seams/include/iralib_interf.h, the C API
header of Iterative Rotations and Assignments (IRA/SOFI): Apache-2.0,
Copyright Miha Gunde and contributors, as its header states. The
Apache-2.0 text is the same as src/readcon-core/vendor/cfg-if/LICENSE-APACHE.

The source package also carries readcon-core, the Rust crates it builds
with, and chemfiles with the libraries chemfiles bundles. rustc also
links its standard library, compiler-rt, and the crates listed after the
vendor set into libreadcon_core.a. Those sources ship with the compiler.
Their licence texts are in inst/licenses/readcon-sysroot. Each part keeps
its own licence, listed below with the path of its licence text inside
the source package. Where a part offers a choice ("A OR B"), dseams
redistributes it under either. Where a part requires every licence
("A AND B"), dseams redistributes it under all of them. inst/AUTHORS
names the authors of each part.

Every licence here allows the source package to be copied and
redistributed alongside MIT code, on these conditions:

- All parts: keep the copyright notices and licence texts. The BSD,
  NCSA and similar licences also forbid using a holder's name to
  endorse a derived product.
- gemmi (MPL-2.0): its files stay under MPL-2.0, and whoever ships
  them, also inside a binary, makes their source available under
  MPL-2.0.
- molfiles/src/abinitplugin.c: its author allows the code "for as long
  as it is part of an open source project" and does "not allow" it "to
  be part of any closed source project". The bundled build compiles it.
  The text is quoted at the end of this file.
- molfiles/src/ply.c and molfiles/src/ply.h: permission is given on
  condition "that you do not sell the software". They ship in the
  archive but are not compiled. The text is quoted at the end of this
  file.
- molfiles/src/hoomdplugin.c and molfiles/src/plyplugin.cxx carry a
  copyright header but no licence statement: hoomdplugin.c Copyright
  2009 Axel Kohlmeyer; plyplugin.cxx Copyright 1995-2016 The Board of
  Trustees of the University of Illinois. Both come from the VMD
  molfile plugins as vendored by chemfiles
  (https://github.com/chemfiles/molfiles) and are distributed under
  molfiles/LICENSE (UIUC Open Source License). plyplugin.cxx includes
  ply.c, so the ply.c "not sold" condition applies if plyplugin is
  ever compiled. It is not in the compiled list today.
- molfiles/src/tngplugin.cxx: BSD, as its header states; the terms are
  those of the TNG library, quoted at the end of this file. It ships in
  the archive and is not compiled.
- tng/ci-scripts/copyright.py, tng/ci-scripts/copyright.sh and
  tng/ci-scripts/clang-format.sh carry TNG's Revised BSD notice and also
  the GROMACS header they were copied from, which is LGPL-2.1-or-later;
  tng/ci-scripts/git-pre-commit carries only that GROMACS header.
  Redistributing them under the LGPL means keeping that notice and the
  licence text available. They are developer scripts
  that ship in the archive and are not compiled or run.
- molfiles/src/ReadPARM.h and molfiles/src/ReadPARM7.h: their notice is
  kept unmodified and any change to the rest of the file is noted in
  comments. The bundled build compiles them (parm and parm7 plugins).

dseams is open source (MIT) and is not sold, which meets the ABINIT and
Georgia Tech conditions. They bind anyone who redistributes dseams too:
the source package, and any build of it that contains the ABINIT
plugin, may not become part of closed-source software, and the source
package, which contains ply.c, may not be sold.


